{{Politics of Romania}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Romania]] in 2009. They were the sixth presidential elections held in [[Romanian Revolution|post-1989]] Romania. The first round took place on 22 November, with a [[Two-round system|run-off round]] between the top two candidates [[Traian Băsescu]] (supported by the [[Democratic Liberal Party (Romania)|Democratic Liberal Party]] or PDL for short) and [[Mircea Geoană]] (supported by the [[Social Democratic Party (Romania)|Social Democratic Party]] or PSD for short) on 6 December 2009. Although most exit polls suggested a victory for Geoană in the runoff, the authorities declared Băsescu the narrow winner with 50.33% of the votes.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.gandul.ro/politica/tariceanu-datele-problemei-sunt-cu-totul-altele-fiecare-partid-are-un-interes-propriu-despre-refuzul-lui-antonescu-de-a-discuta-cu-pd-l-e-bine-sa-avem-si-pareri-personale-5158680|title=Tăriceanu: "Datele problemei sunt cu totul altele. Fiecare partid are un interes propriu". Despre refuzul lui Antonescu de a discuta cu PD-L: "E bine să avem și păreri personale"|date=23 April 2012}}</ref><ref name="jurnalul1">{{Cite web|url=https://jurnalul.ro/stiri/politica/rezultate-finale-bec-basescu-presedinte-cu-50-33-din-voturi-529659.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170826025958/http://jurnalul.ro/stire-alegeri-2009/bec-rezultate-finale-neomologate-ora-14-50-traian-basescu-50-33-mircea-geoana-49-66-529659.html|url-status=dead|title=Rezultate finale BEC: Băsescu, preşedinte cu 50,33% din voturi|archivedate=26 August 2017|website=jurnalul.ro}}</ref> To date, it is the closest presidential election in the political history of Romania.

The opposition contested the results, citing a "high number of void ballots, modified voting protocols, and massive electoral tourism", vowing to challenge the result in the [[Constitutional Court of Romania|constitutional court]].<ref name="SofiaEcho">{{cite web|author=Alex Bivol |url=http://www.sofiaecho.com/2009/12/07/826991_romania-incumbent-president-basescu-wins-second-term |title=Romania incumbent president Basescu wins second term – South Eastern Europe |publisher=The Sofia Echo |access-date=7 December 2009}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8399334.stm|title=Romanian opposition alleges fraud|date=7 December 2009|via=news.bbc.co.uk}}</ref> The [[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe]] (OSCE) declared that the election "was held generally in line with OSCE commitments",<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/politics|title=Bloomberg Politics|website=Bloomberg.com}}</ref> but also urged the authorities to investigate claims of fraud.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.irishtimes.com/news/romania-divided-and-paralysed-by-president-s-disputed-wafer-thin-win-1.786051|title=Romania divided and paralysed by president's disputed, wafer-thin win|first=Daniel|last=McLaughlin|newspaper=The Irish Times}}</ref> On 8 December, the Social Democratic Party (PSD) submitted a request to annul and repeat the run-off to the [[Constitutional Court of Romania|Constitutional Court]], claiming it had been rigged.<ref name="romaniantimes.at">{{Cite web |url=http://www.romaniantimes.at/news/General_News/2009-12-08/4862/Geoana_demands_repeat_of_presidential_elections |title=Geoana demands repeat of presidential elections |access-date=8 December 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091213064842/http://www.romaniantimes.at/news/General_News/2009-12-08/4862/Geoana_demands_repeat_of_presidential_elections |archive-date=13 December 2009 |url-status=dead  }}</ref> On 14 December, the Constitutional Court rejected the request after recounting all the annulled votes.<ref name="GBrown">{{Cite web|date=|title=EVZ.ro – Gordon Brown: Băsescu trebuie să continue reforma în justiţie|url=http://www.evz.ro/articole/detalii-articol/879858/G-Brown-Basescu-trebuie-sa-continue-reforma-in-justitie/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091218172104/http://www.evz.ro/articole/detalii-articol/879858/G-Brown-Basescu-trebuie-sa-continue-reforma-in-justitie/|archive-date=2009-12-18|access-date=2021-12-16|website=www.evz.ro}}</ref>

A [[2009 Romanian parliamentary reform referendum|referendum was held]] alongside the first round of voting on introducing a [[Unicameralism|unicameral parliament]] of up to 300 deputies (replacing the existing [[Bicameralism|bicameral parliament]]) and reducing the number of MPs to 300 but retaining the bicameral structure.

== Candidates ==

There were twelve candidates of which three ran as independents.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.bec2009p.ro/candidati.html |title={{in lang{{!}}ro}} Romanian Central Electoral Commission |access-date=14 November 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091015010037/http://www.bec2009p.ro/candidati.html |archive-date=15 October 2009 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The candidates of the major parties were: the then incumbent [[Traian Băsescu]] ([[Independent politician|independent]], but supported by [[Democratic Liberal Party (Romania)|Democratic Liberal Party]] (PDL) and the official fraction of [[Christian Democratic National Peasants' Party]] (PNȚCD), led by [[Marian Petre Miluț|Marian Miluț]]), [[Mircea Geoană]] ([[Social Democratic Party (Romania)|Social Democratic Party]] or PSD for short), [[Crin Antonescu]] ([[National Liberal Party (Romania)|National Liberal Party]] (PNL) and the contender faction of PNȚCD, led by [[Radu Sîrbu]]), [[Hunor Kelemen]] ([[Democratic Alliance of Hungarians in Romania]] or UDMR/RMDSZ for short)<ref>{{in lang|ro}} [http://www.mediafax.ro/politic/kelemen-hunor-a-fost-votat-drept-candidat-al-udmr-la-presedinti.html?1687;4600774 "Kelemen Hunor a fost votat drept candidat al UDMR la Preşedinţi" ("Hunor Kelemen Voted UDMR Presidential Candidate")], Mediafax, 27 June 2009</ref> and [[Corneliu Vadim Tudor]] ([[Greater Romania Party]] or PRM for short).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.setimes.com/cocoon/setimes/xhtml/en_GB/features/setimes/newsbriefs/2009/04/03/nb-03 |title=Romania's Geoana announces presidential candidacy |publisher=SETimes.com |access-date=7 December 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.setimes.com/cocoon/setimes/xhtml/en_GB/features/setimes/newsbriefs/2009/05/11/nb-08 |title=Romania's Antonescu, Tudor join presidential race |publisher=SETimes.com |access-date=7 December 2009}}</ref> [[Mayor of Bucharest]] [[Sorin Oprescu]] (formerly PSD member) announced his candidacy as an independent candidate on 5 October 2009;<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.setimes.com/cocoon/setimes/xhtml/en_GB/newsbriefs/setimes/newsbriefs/2009/10/06/nb-06 |title=Bucharest mayor Sorin Oprescu enters presidential race |publisher=SETimes.com |access-date=7 December 2009}}</ref> Băsescu had also been mayor of Bucharest before becoming president during the early 2000s.<ref>{{cite web|author=posten |url=http://derstandard.at/fs/1246543132952/Bukarests-Buergermeister-Oprescu-ueberlegt-Praesidentschaftskandidatur |title=Bukarests Bürgermeister Oprescu überlegt Präsidentschaftskandidatur – Rumänien – derStandard.at " International |publisher=Derstandard.at |access-date=7 December 2009}}</ref>

[[Prince Radu of Romania]], husband of [[Margareta of Romania]] first announced his candidacy to the office of [[President of Romania|President]] on 9 April 2009,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.princeradu.ro/news/2303/17/Principele-Radu---primul-candidat-oficial-la-presedintia-Romaniei|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090412064112/http://www.princeradu.ro/news/2303/17/Principele-Radu---primul-candidat-oficial-la-presedintia-Romaniei|url-status=dead|title=Prince Radu, the first official candidate to the office of President of Romania|archivedate=12 April 2009}}</ref> but later withdrew.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.princeradu.ro/news/2890/17/Declaratie-de-Presa-02-Septembrie-2009|title=Official site|access-date=2 September 2009|archive-date=5 September 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090905155343/http://www.princeradu.ro/news/2890/17/Declaratie-de-Presa-02-Septembrie-2009|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.princeradublog.ro/atitudini/statement-of-prince-radu-of-romania/|title=princeradublog.ro|website=www.princeradublog.ro}}</ref>

