[[File:Presidential election 2009 second round.png|300px|thumb|right|The results of the second voting round by county and in the Bucharest municipality]][[File:2009-romania-presidential-second.png|thumb|298x298px|The results of the second voting round by communes (some commune borders are inaccurate)]]Both runoff candidates declared themselves winners after tight exit polls.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/12/07/world/europe/07romania.html|title=Victory Claimed by Both Candidates in Romania (Published 2009)|first=Nicholas|last=Kulish|newspaper=The New York Times|date=6 December 2009}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.gandul.info/politica/geoana-51-6-basescu-48-4-insomar-ccsb-g-51-2-b-48-8-csop-g-50-b-50-curs-g-50-8-b-49-2-basescu-si-geoana-s-au-declarat-invingatori-5156935|title=Vezi cum arătau exit poll-urile, duminică, la încheierea votului pentru președintele României. Citește Live Textul din ziua alegerilor – Gândul|date=23 April 2012}}</ref> On Monday, 7 December 2009 at 8&nbsp;am, BEC published the first official partial results, after having counted the votes from 95.4% of the total of 21706 poll stations. According to these partial results, [[Traian Băsescu]] had achieved 50.43% of the total eligible votes, while [[Mircea Geoană]] got the rest of 49.57%.<ref name="partials8">{{Cite web|url=http://www.bec2009p.ro/Documente+PDF/Rezultate/Rezultate+partiale+turul+II/partiale+ora8.pdf|title=bec2009p.ro|website=www.bec2009p.ro}}</ref> The official partial results tallied at 10:16&nbsp;am (Monday, 7 December 2009) attested that [[Traian Băsescu]] achieved 50.37% of the total eligible votes and [[Mircea Geoană]] 49.62%.<ref name="partials10">{{Cite web|url=http://www.bec2009p.ro/Documente+PDF/Rezultate/Rezultate+partiale+turul+II/partiale+ora10.pdf|title=bec2009p.ro|website=www.bec2009p.ro}}</ref> A majority of 79% the votes from abroad, fully counted in this tally, went for Băsescu (115,831 to 31,045).<ref name="auto" /><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://jurnalul.ro/stiri/politica/basescu-pe-primul-loc-la-votul-in-italia-spania-sua-geoana-castiga-in-rusia-belarus-si-teatre-529684.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303220308/http://jurnalul.ro/stire-alegeri-2009/basescu-pe-primul-loc-la-votul-in-italia-spania-sua-geoana-castiga-in-rusia-belarus-si-teatre-529684.html|url-status=dead|title=Băsescu, pe primul loc la votul în Italia, Spania, SUA; Geoană câştigă în Rusia, Belarus şi teatre|archivedate=3 March 2016|website=jurnalul.ro}}</ref> Some 94% of the estimated 200,000 [[Moldova]]ns with Romanian citizenship who voted in the election cast their vote for Băsescu, who has been a vocal advocate of Moldovans' right to regain Romanian citizenship.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.rferl.org/a/Moldovan_PM_Satisfied_With_Romanian_Vote_Result/1897332.html|title=Moldovan PM 'Satisfied' With Romanian Vote Result|newspaper=Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty|date=8 December 2009 }}</ref> Following final results, [[Sibiu]] mayor [[Klaus Iohannis]] announced that he is abandoning his bid to become prime minister in the current situation.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hotnews.ro/stiri-politic-6685693-klaus-johannis-parcursul-meu-posibil-premier-terminat-aici-sunt-raman-sibiu.htm|title=Klaus Johannis: Parcursul meu de posibil premier s-a terminat aici. Sunt si raman in Sibiu – Politic – HotNews.ro|date=7 December 2009|website=www.hotnews.ro}}</ref> Romanian stock markets fell to a four-month low after the official results were announced, deepening a political crisis that threatens ties with international lenders.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=20601085&sid=avb0O2xlEfG4|title=Bloomberg Politics – Bloomberg|website=Bloomberg.com}}</ref>

